Z||000000||fiero_nightmare||11-09-2001||12:54 AM||||i have a 85 GT 4 speed and last night i took off the slave cylinder and shift cable bracket so i could put some new bolt in because 2 of them were rounded off and wouldn't tighten down right. i got it all back together 2 hours ago and decided to take it for a drive. after i got it started i went to put it into gear and it wouldn't go into ANY gear and ground when i tried to put it in reverse. i tried to adjust the shifter cables and it didn't help. when the engine wasn't running it would go into any gear easy so i put it in first then started it. when i first hit the key the car jerked forward then started. now after that it will go into any gear easily.  i am afraid to drive it because something might go wrong and i will have to have it towed.  <P>anyone have any idea as to what the problem is/was?<P><BR>there is one more thing. is the spedo gear supposed to have a retainer on it or something? mine doesn't and it has falled in the tranni twice.<P>------------------<BR>my 85 GT pics <P>http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw||||reg||5
Z||000001||SLYGUY||11-09-2001||03:53 AM||||When you reinstalled the slave you must of misaligned the rod that exits it. When you push in the clutch that rod is what is disengaging your clutch. Either it is misaligned (you didn't describe what the clutch felt like when you push it) or you have a few hours of bleeding to do. <P>If it is the latter, a Mityvac brake bleeder pump makes the job much easier and faster. <BR>How did the clutch pedal feel after the work you did? Mushy? Similar to how it felt before but now the clutch engages/disengages at the floor?<BR>Good luck with it. Keep us posted.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||11-09-2001||03:57 AM||||Be sure to check out Archie's procedure for bleeding the infamous Fiero clutch hydraulic system!! <BR> <A HREF="http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A>  <P><BR>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero_nightmare||11-09-2001||07:28 AM||||thanks for the reply's. the clutch felt normal when i engaged/disengaged it. i did notice however that the fluid was a bit low but i don't see any leaks anywhere. when i reinstalled the slave cylinder i put the rod in the round hole. this is the first time i ever did anything like this on a car with a manual tranni so im not used to these things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||11-09-2001||08:58 PM||||My best guess:<P>When you removed the slave cylinder, the piston ran to the end of its bore, and the old stiff piston seal slid across all of the rust and crud that accumulates in the bore where the piston normally never runs.  This disrupted contact between the seal and the bore, and allowed fluid to escape and air to enter.<P>That's my story, and I'm sticking to it!  If true, you need to replace the slave cylinder.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 11-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||11-10-2001||02:19 AM||||Hear, hear!||||reg||1
